Constant Value Where <code>DrillFlowCore.scriptedReplyDelayNanoseconds</code> 400,000,000 ns (0.4s) pause between an accepted mid-line move and the scripted opponent reply <code>DrillAutoAdvancePolicy.correctDelayNanoseconds</code> 900,000,000 ns (0.9s) auto-advance delay after <code>.correct</code> <code>DrillAutoAdvancePolicy.reviewDelayNanoseconds</code> 1,600,000,000 ns (1.6s) auto-advance delay after <code>.closeEnough</code> / <code>.wrong</code> <p><code>DrillAutoAdvancePolicy.delayNanoseconds(for:)</code> returns <code>nil</code> — no schedule at all — for <code>.checking</code>, <code>.unavailable</code>, <code>.done</code>, and <code>nil</code>.</p> <p><code>DrillAutoAdvanceTicket</code> is the public counterpart to the core's own internal <code>AutoAdvanceTicket</code> (above), for a caller that paces its own advance directly against <code>DrillAutoAdvancePolicy</code> instead of driving it through <code>DrillFlowCore</code>'s effect vocabulary. It carries the same two facts an ownership check needs — an <code>itemIndex</code> and a <code>feedbackGeneration</code> <code>UUID</code> — captured before the delay and compared after it:</p> <pre><code>let ticket = DrillAutoAdvanceTicket(itemIndex: index, feedbackGeneration: generation)
guard let delay = DrillAutoAdvancePolicy.delayNanoseconds(for: verdict) else { return }
try await Task.sleep(nanoseconds: delay)
guard ticket.isCurrent(itemIndex: currentIndex, feedbackGeneration: currentGeneration)
else { return }
</code></pre> <p>Checking cancellation alone is not enough — a cancelled task can already be resuming, which is precisely when it would advance past an item the user has since moved on from; <code>isCurrent</code> is the check that catches that case.</p>

Method Scope Default <code>maximumKeys</code> <code>storedJSONKeysReportingError</code> flat <code>*.json</code> files directly in the blob root <code>maximumEnumeratedJSONKeys</code> <code>storedAuthoredJSONKeysReportingError</code> <code>authored-heads/</code> + <code>authored-revisions/</code> + flat legacy authored keys <code>maximumEnumeratedJSONKeys</code> <code>storedEndgameMiningObjectsReportingError</code> the whole <code>endgame-mining/</code> subtree <code>maximumEnumeratedJSONKeys</code> <code>storedEndgameMiningV1HeadKeysReportingError</code> fixed <code>personalEndgames/head/v1[/&lt;replica&gt;]</code> subtree only <code>EndgameMiningGenerationResolver.maximumHeadCandidates</code> (4,096) <code>storedEndgameMiningV2HeadKeysReportingError</code> fixed <code>personalEndgames/head/v2[/&lt;replica&gt;]</code> subtree only <code>EndgameMiningV2GenerationResolver.maximumHeadCandidates</code> (4,096) <code>snapshotJSONByKey(excludingKeys:includingAuthoredRecords:)</code> full-directory export, <code>[String: String]</code> derived from the two enumerations above <p><code>maximumEnumeratedJSONKeys = 1_000_000</code> is the shared safety ceiling behind nearly every one of these. It exists so a hostile or damaged namespace can't turn a bounded inventory pass into an unbounded per-edit decode. Every result type here (<code>FileJSONBlobKeyListResult</code>, <code>FileJSONBlobStoredObjectListResult</code>, <code>FileJSONBlobSnapshotResult</code>) is deliberately all-or-nothing: exceeding the ceiling — or hitting any other structural problem, like a symlink where a directory is expected — fails the entire call rather than returning a truncated list. A truncated list would let a backup or a GC pass believe it had seen everything.</p> <p><code>snapshotJSONByKey</code> is the concrete case that exercises this: it unions <code>storedJSONKeysReportingError</code> with <code>storedAuthoredJSONKeysReportingError</code> (when <code>includingAuthoredRecords</code> is true), excludes the caller's own backup/library envelope keys plus the authored- and endgame-mining infrastructure keys, then reads each remaining key. If a key that was just enumerated disappears before its read (a real race, not a bug), the whole snapshot fails with <code>"file disappeared during backup"</code> — a full backup cannot truthfully claim completeness in that window.</p> <pre><code>let store = FileJSONBlobStore()

switch store.loadJSONReportingError(forKey: TacticsRatingStore.blobKey) {
case .value(let json):
    print(json)
case .missing:
    print("no rating blob yet — a fresh Self() is fine")
case .failure(let message):
    print("unreadable, do NOT treat this as missing: \(message)")
}

switch store.snapshotJSONByKey(excludingKeys: ["library.fullbackup.v1"]) {
case .value(let blobsByKey):
    // write blobsByKey into a backup archive — every key it saw is present
    break
case .failure(let message):
    // never write a partial backup
    print("backup aborted: \(message)")
}
</code></pre>

Store Cap applied to the merged result Backing constant <code>TacticsPerformanceStore</code> <code>.capLast(3_000)</code> on <code>attempts</code> <code>TacticsPerformanceStore.maxAttempts</code> <code>RepertoireDrillStats</code> <code>Array(attempts.suffix(200))</code> per repertoire matches <code>RepertoireDrillStats.maxAttemptsPerRep</code> (private to that type — the merger repeats the same <code>200</code>) <p>Both caps exist so a merge of two already-large histories doesn't itself produce a blob over CloudKit's ~1 MB record limit.</p> <pre><code>let merged = ProgressBlobSemanticMerger.mergedResult(
    forKey: TacticsRatingStore.blobKey,
    candidates: [
        .init(json: localJSON, updatedAt: localUpdatedAt),
        .init(json: cloudJSON, updatedAt: cloudUpdatedAt),
    ]
)
if let merged {
    store.saveJSON(merged.json, forKey: TacticsRatingStore.blobKey)
    print(merged.contributors.count, "replicas folded in")
    print(merged.latestContributorUpdatedAt)   // max(updatedAt) over contributors
}
</code></pre>
